L.push({id:"f8", unit:"F3", title:"Oak, Malolactic and Lees", mins:13,
summary:"The three big winemaking signatures you'll smell in the exam glass.",
body:`
<h3>Oak</h3>
<p>Oak does three jobs: adds flavor (if new), allows slow oxygen exchange (softening tannins, stabilizing color), and adds tannin. Variables:</p>
<ul><li><b>Species:</b> French oak (<i>Quercus petraea, robur</i>) is tighter-grained, giving subtle spice, cedar, toast. American oak (<i>Q. alba</i>) is sawn rather than split and gives bold vanilla, coconut and dill. Eastern European (Hungarian, Slavonian) is often in between; Slavonian casks are traditional in Barolo and Brunello.</li>
<li><b>Size:</b> 225 L barrique (Bordeaux) or 228 L pièce (Burgundy); bigger casks (botti, foudres) impart less flavor.</li>
<li><b>Toast:</b> light, medium, heavy. More toast = more smoke, coffee, mocha.</li>
<li><b>Age:</b> new oak gives the most flavor; after 3-4 uses a barrel is neutral.</li>
<li><b>Alternatives:</b> chips and staves, cheap and fast, oak flavor without oxygen.</li></ul>
<p>Tasting clue: dill and coconut are the giveaway of American oak, which is why Rioja Reserva and many Zinfandels are recognizable blind.</p>
<h3>Malolactic conversion (MLC)</h3>
<p>Lactic acid bacteria convert sharp malic acid (green apple) into softer lactic acid (milk). Result: lower acidity, creamy texture, and buttery aromas from <b>diacetyl</b>. Nearly all reds go through MLC. For whites it is a style choice: yes for Meursault and buttery Californian Chardonnay; usually blocked for Riesling, Sauvignon Blanc and Albariño.</p>
<h3>Lees</h3>
<p>Lees are dead yeast cells. <b>Lees aging</b> (sur lie) adds bready, biscuity flavors and creamy texture. <b>Bâtonnage</b> (stirring) increases the effect. Muscadet Sèvre-et-Maine sur Lie is the textbook example in a cheap bottle; Champagne is the luxury version.</p>
<h3>The "butter, toast, vanilla" question</h3>
<p>Classic WSET 25-mark question: "Name two winemaking techniques that give butter, toast and vanilla." The answer is <b>MLC</b> (butter, from diacetyl) and <b>new oak maturation</b> (toast and vanilla from toasted barrels). Each would earn about 5 marks when explained: what happens, why it creates the aroma, and an example wine.</p>
<h3>How a barrel is made, and what that costs</h3>
<p>Oak for barrels comes from trees 100 to 200 years old. French forests are named on cooperage lists: Allier, Tronçais, Nevers and the Vosges give tight-grained wood for wine; wide-grained <b>Limousin</b> goes mostly to Cognac. The staves are stacked outdoors for two to three years so rain washes out harsh tannin, then bent over a fire and toasted. French oak must be split along the grain; American oak can be sawn, which wastes less wood and is one reason it costs about half as much.</p>
<p>A new French barrique costs roughly €800 to €1,000 and holds 300 bottles. All-new oak therefore adds about €3 to every bottle before anything else is paid for. Oak is a budget decision as much as a style one.</p>
<h3>The dials a winemaker turns</h3>
<table class="t"><tr><th>Wine</th><th>Typical oak regime</th></tr>
<tr><td>Chablis</td><td>Steel or old oak; little or no oak flavor</td></tr>
<tr><td>Village white Burgundy</td><td>Barrel-fermented, about 20 to 30% new, 10 to 12 months</td></tr>
<tr><td>Classed-growth Bordeaux, Napa Cabernet</td><td>18 to 22 months in barriques, 50 to 100% new French</td></tr>
<tr><td>Rioja Gran Reserva (traditional)</td><td>Years in old American oak: gentle oxidation, vanilla and coconut</td></tr>
<tr><td>Barolo (traditional)</td><td>Two years or more in large old botti: oxygen without oak flavor</td></tr>
<tr><td>Barossa Shiraz (traditional)</td><td>American oak hogsheads</td></tr></table>
<p><b>Barrel fermentation</b> and barrel aging are not the same. A white fermented in barrel tastes <i>less</i> oaky than one fermented in tank and then put in barrel, because yeast take up and transform oak compounds and the lees buffer the wood. That is why good white Burgundy carries so much new oak so quietly.</p>
<p>Wine evaporates through the staves (the "angel's share"), so barrels are topped up every few weeks. A barrel left with headspace oxidizes: a fault in table wine, the whole point in Oloroso and Vin Jaune. <b>Micro-oxygenation</b> bubbles tiny amounts of oxygen through wine in tank to mimic a barrel's softening at lower cost.</p>
<h3>Malolactic conversion: the fine print</h3>
<ul><li>It usually follows the alcoholic fermentation. In a cold Burgundy cellar it may not finish until spring.</li>
<li>Winemakers block it by chilling, adding SO₂ and filtering out the bacteria.</li>
<li><b>Partial</b> conversion, or blending lots that did and didn't, tunes the acidity. Many Champagne houses and warm-climate Chardonnay producers do this.</li>
<li>It makes wine more stable: no malic acid is left for bacteria to attack in bottle.</li>
<li>Butter depends on the bacteria and on timing. Wine left on its lees after conversion loses diacetyl, which is why Meursault is less buttery than old-style California Chardonnay.</li></ul>
<h3>Lees, gross and fine</h3>
<p><b>Gross lees</b> are the heavy sediment that falls in the first days: grape solids and dead yeast. They are usually racked off because they can turn foul. <b>Fine lees</b> settle slowly afterwards and are the ones kept. They add texture, and they absorb oxygen, which protects the wine but can tip it into <b>reduction</b>: the struck-match note that marks many modern white Burgundies. Stirring brings in a little air and richness; leaving lees unstirred keeps the wine tighter.</p>
<h3>Reading it in the glass</h3>
<table class="t"><tr><th>You notice</th><th>It points to</th></tr>
<tr><td>Vanilla, toast, clove, smoke</td><td>New French oak</td></tr>
<tr><td>Coconut, dill, sweet vanilla</td><td>American oak</td></tr>
<tr><td>Butter, cream, softer acid</td><td>Malolactic conversion</td></tr>
<tr><td>Bread dough, biscuit, creamy texture</td><td>Lees aging</td></tr>
<tr><td>Struck match, flint</td><td>Reductive handling on lees</td></tr>
<tr><td>Nuts, dried fruit, amber color</td><td>Deliberate oxidation</td></tr></table>`,
floor:`<p>The CMS 2024 grid splits non-fruit into "from the grape" and "from winemaking." Winemaking descriptors: bread dough, yeast, butter, cream, yogurt, toast, marzipan, stems. Oak gets its own block: new oak yes/no, intensity, descriptors, and type (French, American, Eastern European).</p>`,
analyst:`<p>In the SAT these are <b>secondary aromas</b>: yeast (biscuit, bread, brioche), MLC (butter, cream, cheese), oak (vanilla, cloves, coconut, cedar, smoke, chocolate). Tertiary aromas come from aging: leather, earth, mushroom, honey, nuts.</p>`,
terms:[["Barrique","225-liter Bordeaux barrel."],["MLC","Malolactic conversion: malic acid to lactic acid, softening wine."],["Diacetyl","Buttery compound produced during MLC."],["Bâtonnage","Stirring the lees in barrel."],["Sur lie","Aged on the lees, as in Muscadet."],["Seasoning","Air-drying oak staves outdoors for two to three years."],["Angel's share","Wine lost to evaporation through the barrel."],["Topping up","Refilling barrels to replace evaporated wine and keep air out."],["Micro-oxygenation","Dosing tiny amounts of oxygen into wine in tank."],["Gross lees","The heavy first sediment after fermentation, usually removed."],["Fine lees","The light yeast sediment kept for texture and protection."]],
quiz:[
[1,"Dill and coconut aromas suggest:","American oak",["French oak","Stainless steel","Concrete"],"American Quercus alba is rich in whiskey lactones."],
[1,"Malolactic conversion turns malic acid into:","Lactic acid",["Tartaric acid","Citric acid","Acetic acid"],"Lactic acid is softer, giving a creamier wine."],
[2,"Which two techniques give butter, toast and vanilla in Chardonnay?","MLC and new oak maturation",["Carbonic maceration and lees aging","Cold fermentation and sterile filtration","Chaptalization and fining"],"MLC creates diacetyl; toasted new barrels give toast and vanilla."],
[2,"A barrel's flavor contribution becomes negligible after roughly:","3-4 uses",["One use","10 uses","20 years"],"Older barrels still allow oxygen exchange."],
[1,"Muscadet 'sur lie' is aged on:","Dead yeast cells",["Toasted oak chips","Pressed grape skins","A layer of flor"],"Lees add texture and a light bready note."],
[3,"Traditional Barolo producers favor which vessel?","Large Slavonian oak botti",["New American barriques","Concrete eggs only","Amphora"],"Large casks give oxygen without overt oak flavor; modernists used barriques."],
[2,"Why are oak staves left outdoors for two to three years?","Rain washes out harsh tannin",["To deepen their color","To raise the vanilla content","To kill woodworm"],"Seasoning softens the wood's raw tannin before the barrel is built."],
[2,"Wide-grained Limousin oak is used mainly for:","Cognac",["Champagne","White Burgundy","Sherry"],"Tight-grained Allier, Tronçais and Vosges oak is preferred for wine."],
[3,"A white fermented in barrel, compared with one only aged in barrel, tastes:","Less oaky",["More oaky","More tannic","More oxidized"],"Yeast take up oak compounds and the lees buffer the wood."],
[2,"Blocking malolactic conversion keeps a white wine:","Sharper and more fruity",["Softer and buttery","Sweeter","Lower in alcohol"],"Malic acid stays, so the wine tastes crisper: Riesling, Sauvignon Blanc."],
[2,"A struck-match note in white Burgundy comes from:","Reduction on the lees",["New American oak","Malolactic conversion","Botrytis"],"Fine lees absorb oxygen; with little air the wine turns reductive."],
[2,"Micro-oxygenation is used to:","Soften wine in tank",["Carbonate sparkling wine","Stop fermentation","Remove tartrates"],"Tiny doses of oxygen mimic the slow air exchange of a barrel."]
]});

L.push({id:"f9", unit:"F3", title:"Sparkling Wine Methods", mins:14,
summary:"Traditional, transfer, ancestral, tank, Asti and carbonation, plus dosage terms.",
body:`
<p>Bubbles are trapped CO₂ from a second fermentation (or, for cheap wine, injected). How and where that second fermentation happens decides the style.</p>
<h3>Traditional method (méthode champenoise)</h3>
<ol><li>Make a high-acid, low-alcohol base wine, often blending villages and vintages (the <b>assemblage</b>).</li>
<li>Bottle it with <b>liqueur de tirage</b>: wine, sugar, yeast and nutrients. Seal with a crown cap.</li>
<li>The second fermentation in bottle creates about 5-6 atmospheres of pressure.</li>
<li><b>Lees aging</b> (autolysis) builds brioche, biscuit, toast. Non-vintage Champagne: minimum 15 months total, 12 on lees. Vintage: 3 years.</li>
<li><b>Riddling</b> (remuage) moves lees to the neck, by hand on pupitres or by gyropalette.</li>
<li><b>Disgorgement:</b> the neck is frozen and the plug of lees is shot out.</li>
<li><b>Dosage:</b> topping up with <b>liqueur d'expédition</b> (wine plus sugar) to set sweetness.</li></ol>
<p>Used for Champagne, Crémant, Cava, Franciacorta, Cap Classique, English sparkling and most premium New World sparklers.</p>
<h3>The other methods</h3>
<ul><li><b>Transfer:</b> second ferment in bottle, then emptied into a tank, filtered and rebottled. Saves riddling; allows lees character. Common for large or small formats.</li>
<li><b>Ancestral (méthode ancestrale / pét-nat):</b> one fermentation, bottled before it finishes. Often cloudy and gently sparkling. Blanquette de Limoux's ancestral style, modern pét-nat.</li>
<li><b>Tank (Charmat or Martinotti):</b> second fermentation in a pressurized tank, then filtered and bottled. Preserves fresh, fruity aromas. Prosecco.</li>
<li><b>Asti method:</b> a single fermentation in tank, stopped by chilling at about 7-9.5% abv, leaving sweetness and grapey Moscato aroma. Asti and Moscato d'Asti (frizzante).</li>
<li><b>Carbonation:</b> CO₂ injected. Large bubbles that fade fast; inexpensive wines.</li></ul>
<h3>Sweetness (dosage) terms, driest to sweetest</h3>
<table class="t"><tr><th>Term</th><th>g/L sugar</th></tr>
<tr><td>Brut Nature (Zero Dosage)</td><td>0-3</td></tr><tr><td>Extra Brut</td><td>0-6</td></tr><tr><td>Brut</td><td>0-12</td></tr><tr><td>Extra Dry / Extra Sec</td><td>12-17</td></tr><tr><td>Sec</td><td>17-32</td></tr><tr><td>Demi-Sec</td><td>32-50</td></tr><tr><td>Doux</td><td>50+</td></tr></table>
<p>The trap everyone falls for: <b>Extra Dry is sweeter than Brut</b>.</p>
<h3>Why the base wine is thin and sharp</h3>
<p>A good sparkling base tastes austere: about 10 to 11% alcohol, very high acid, little flavor. That is deliberate. The second fermentation adds about 1.2 to 1.5% alcohol, so the base must start low. Bubbles make acidity feel softer and carry aroma upward, so a base that tasted balanced would be flat and heavy once it sparkled. About 24 g/L of sugar in the tirage gives the full six atmospheres.</p>
<h3>Pressure words</h3>
<table class="t"><tr><th>Term</th><th>Pressure</th><th>Examples</th></tr>
<tr><td>Fully sparkling (mousseux, spumante, Sekt)</td><td>3 atmospheres or more; Champagne about 5 to 6</td><td>Champagne, Cava, Prosecco Spumante, Asti</td></tr>
<tr><td>Semi-sparkling (pétillant, frizzante, Perlwein)</td><td>About 1 to 2.5 atmospheres</td><td>Moscato d'Asti, Prosecco Frizzante, most Lambrusco, Vinho Verde's spritz</td></tr></table>
<h3>What the label is telling you</h3>
<ul><li><b>Non-vintage (NV):</b> a blend of years, built for a consistent house style. The bulk of all Champagne.</li>
<li><b>Vintage:</b> one year, made only when the harvest is good enough.</li>
<li><b>Prestige cuvée:</b> a house's top wine (Dom Pérignon, Cristal, Krug Grande Cuvée).</li>
<li><b>Blanc de Blancs:</b> white grapes only, in Champagne meaning Chardonnay. Lean, citrus, chalky.</li>
<li><b>Blanc de Noirs:</b> white wine from black grapes (Pinot Noir, Meunier). Broader, red-fruited.</li>
<li><b>Rosé:</b> usually a little still red wine blended in before the second fermentation.</li>
<li><b>Méthode traditionnelle, Metodo Classico, Méthode Cap Classique:</b> all mean the traditional method. In the US, "fermented in <i>this</i> bottle" means traditional; "fermented in <i>the</i> bottle" means the transfer method.</li></ul>
<h3>The world's sparkling wines at a glance</h3>
<table class="t"><tr><th>Wine</th><th>Method</th><th>Grapes</th><th>Minimum on lees</th></tr>
<tr><td>Champagne</td><td>Traditional</td><td>Chardonnay, Pinot Noir, Meunier</td><td>12 months (15 total) NV; 36 total vintage</td></tr>
<tr><td>Crémant (Alsace, Loire, Bourgogne, Limoux and others)</td><td>Traditional</td><td>Regional</td><td>9 months</td></tr>
<tr><td>Cava</td><td>Traditional</td><td>Macabeo, Xarel·lo, Parellada</td><td>9 months; Reserva 18; Gran Reserva 30</td></tr>
<tr><td>Franciacorta</td><td>Traditional</td><td>Chardonnay, Pinot Nero, Pinot Bianco</td><td>18 months</td></tr>
<tr><td>Trento DOC</td><td>Traditional</td><td>Chardonnay, Pinot Nero</td><td>15 months</td></tr>
<tr><td>Cap Classique</td><td>Traditional</td><td>Often Chardonnay, Pinot Noir</td><td>12 months</td></tr>
<tr><td>English sparkling</td><td>Traditional</td><td>Chardonnay, Pinot Noir, Meunier</td><td>Usually well over a year</td></tr>
<tr><td>Prosecco</td><td>Tank</td><td>Glera</td><td>None required: about 30 days in tank</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sekt</td><td>Mostly tank; Winzersekt is traditional</td><td>Riesling and others</td><td>9 months for traditional</td></tr>
<tr><td>Lambrusco</td><td>Mostly tank</td><td>Lambrusco family</td><td>None</td></tr>
<tr><td>Asti, Moscato d'Asti</td><td>Asti (single tank ferment)</td><td>Moscato Bianco</td><td>None</td></tr></table>
<h3>Telling them apart blind</h3>
<table class="t"><tr><th>You notice</th><th>It points to</th></tr>
<tr><td>Brioche, toast, biscuit; fine, lasting bubbles</td><td>Traditional method with long lees aging</td></tr>
<tr><td>Fresh pear, apple, white flowers; frothy, short-lived bubbles</td><td>Tank method: Prosecco</td></tr>
<tr><td>Grapes, peach, orange blossom; sweet, low alcohol</td><td>Asti method</td></tr>
<tr><td>Earthy, rubbery note with apple; Spanish grapes</td><td>Cava</td></tr>
<tr><td>Cloudy, cidery, gentle fizz</td><td>Ancestral method (pét-nat)</td></tr></table>
<p>Autolysis only becomes obvious after about 15 to 18 months on lees, which is why minimum-aged Cava and Crémant taste fruitier than Champagne, and why a long-aged Franciacorta or English sparkling can pass for it.</p>`,
floor:`<p>Certified-level question pair from the CMS guide: Introductory asks "Liqueur de tirage is used in the production of which beverage?" (Champagne). Certified asks "What ingredients are found in the liqueur de tirage?" (wine, sugar, yeast, nutrients/riddling aid). Same topic, one level deeper.</p>`,
analyst:`<p>WSET Level 3 LO3 covers all six methods. Sample MCQ: "Removing sediment from a bottle of Champagne is called?" Answer: disgorgement. Diploma D4 adds 3 sparkling wines blind and open-response questions on production.</p>`,
terms:[["Liqueur de tirage","Wine, sugar, yeast and nutrients added to trigger second fermentation in bottle."],["Autolysis","Breakdown of dead yeast giving brioche and toast."],["Remuage","Riddling: moving lees into the bottle neck."],["Dégorgement","Disgorgement: removing the frozen lees plug."],["Liqueur d'expédition","Wine and sugar added after disgorgement; sets the dosage."],["Charmat","Tank method used for Prosecco."],["Prestige cuvée","A Champagne house's top wine."],["Blanc de Blancs","Sparkling wine from white grapes only."],["Blanc de Noirs","White sparkling wine from black grapes."],["Frizzante","Italian for semi-sparkling, about 1 to 2.5 atmospheres."],["Winzersekt","German grower sparkling wine made by the traditional method."]],
quiz:[
[1,"Liqueur de tirage is used in the production of:","Champagne",["Bordeaux","Vin Doux Naturel","Vermouth"],"Official CMS Introductory sample question."],
[2,"What ingredients are in liqueur de tirage?","Wine, sugar, yeast and nutrients",["Grape spirit, sugar and old wine","Sugar syrup and sulfur dioxide","Reserve wine and bentonite only"],"Official CMS Certified-level version of the same topic."],
[1,"Which is sweeter?","Extra Dry",["Brut","Extra Brut","Brut Nature"],"Extra Dry is 12-17 g/L; Brut is up to 12 g/L."],
[1,"Prosecco is typically made by which method?","Tank (Charmat/Martinotti)",["Traditional (in bottle)","Transfer (bottle to tank)","Carbonation (injected CO₂)"],"Tank fermentation keeps Glera's fresh pear and floral aromas."],
[2,"Minimum total aging for non-vintage Champagne?","15 months, 12 on lees",["9 months, all on lees","36 months, 24 on lees","6 months, 3 on lees"],"Vintage Champagne requires 3 years."],
[2,"Removing the sediment from a Champagne bottle is called:","Disgorgement",["Riddling","Dosage","Assemblage"],"WSET Level 3 official sample question."],
[2,"Moscato d'Asti's sweetness comes from:","Stopping a single fermentation by chilling",["Adding sugar after the wine ferments dry","Leaving botrytis to concentrate the grapes","Fortifying the must with grape spirit"],"The Asti method leaves residual grape sugar."],
[2,"The second fermentation raises alcohol by about:","1.2 to 1.5%",["0.2 to 0.3%","0.5 to 0.7%","2.5 to 3%"],"So base wines are picked for about 10 to 11% and very high acid."],
[2,"Frizzante and pétillant wines have a pressure of roughly:","1 to 2.5 atmospheres",["3 to 4 atmospheres","5 to 6 atmospheres","8 atmospheres"],"Fully sparkling wine has 3 or more; Champagne about 5 to 6."],
[2,"Blanc de Noirs is:","White wine from black grapes",["Rosé made by blending","White wine from white grapes","Red sparkling wine"],"In Champagne, Pinot Noir and Meunier pressed gently."],
[3,"On a US label, 'fermented in the bottle' indicates:","The transfer method",["The traditional method","The tank method","Carbonation"],"'Fermented in this bottle' is the traditional method."],
[2,"Which wine requires the longest minimum time on lees?","Franciacorta",["Cava","Crémant de Loire","Prosecco"],"Franciacorta 18 months; Cava and Crémant 9; Prosecco none."],
[2,"Pear and white flowers with frothy bubbles and no toast suggest:","Tank method",["Long lees aging","Ancestral method","Transfer method"],"The tank method preserves primary fruit: Prosecco."]
]});

L.push({id:"f10", unit:"F3", title:"Sweet and Fortified Wines", mins:15,
summary:"Concentrating sugar, stopping fermentation, and building strength.",
body:`
<h3>How sweet wines are made</h3>
<p>Two families: <b>stop fermentation early</b> (chill, filter, add SO₂, or fortify), or <b>concentrate the sugar</b> so yeast can't finish (or add back sweet juice, called <b>süssreserve</b> in Germany).</p>
<ul><li><b>Noble rot:</b> Sauternes and Barsac (Sémillon, Sauvignon Blanc, Muscadelle), Tokaji Aszú (Furmint), German BA and TBA. Honey, apricot, saffron, marmalade.</li>
<li><b>Late harvest:</b> grapes left to overripen and shrivel. Alsace Vendanges Tardives, German Spätlese and Auslese.</li>
<li><b>Freezing:</b> grapes picked frozen; water stays as ice in the press. Icewine (Canada, from Vidal, Riesling, Cabernet Franc) and Eiswein (Germany, Austria). Must be naturally frozen.</li>
<li><b>Drying (passito):</b> grapes dried on mats or racks. Amarone (fermented dry) and Recioto (sweet) in Valpolicella, Vin Santo in Tuscany, Passito di Pantelleria. Vin de paille in Jura.</li></ul>
<h3>Fortified wines</h3>
<p>Fortification means adding grape spirit. <i>When</i> you add it decides everything:</p>
<ul><li><b>During fermentation</b> keeps natural grape sugar: Port, Madeira (most), Vins Doux Naturels (Banyuls, Maury, Muscat de Beaumes-de-Venise, Rivesaltes).</li>
<li><b>After fermentation</b> gives a dry base, sweetened later if at all: Sherry.</li></ul>
<h3>Biological versus oxidative aging</h3>
<p>Sherry introduces the most important split in fortified wine. Under a layer of yeast called <b>flor</b>, wine is protected from oxygen and becomes pale, tangy and saline (Fino, Manzanilla). Fortify past about 17% and flor dies; the wine ages <b>oxidatively</b>, becoming brown, nutty, walnut and toffee (Oloroso). Amontillado and Palo Cortado sit between. This also explains Tawny versus Ruby Port: oxidative wood aging versus reductive, fruit-preserving aging. Full details are in the Iberia unit.</p>
<h3>Madeira's heat</h3>
<p>Madeira is deliberately heated: <b>estufagem</b> (tanks warmed to about 113-122°F / 45-50°C for at least 3 months) for commercial wines, or <b>canteiro</b> (casks resting in warm lofts for years) for the best. The result is nearly indestructible: an opened bottle lasts months.</p>
<h3>How sweet is sweet?</h3>
<table class="t"><tr><th>Wine</th><th>Typical residual sugar</th></tr>
<tr><td>Moscato d'Asti</td><td>About 100 to 150 g/L, at 5 to 6% alcohol</td></tr>
<tr><td>Port</td><td>About 90 to 120 g/L</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sauternes</td><td>120 to 150 g/L</td></tr>
<tr><td>Tokaji Aszú</td><td>At least 120 g/L by law</td></tr>
<tr><td>Icewine, Trockenbeerenauslese</td><td>Often 160 to 250 g/L</td></tr>
<tr><td>Pedro Ximénez Sherry</td><td>400 g/L and more</td></tr>
<tr><td>Tokaji Eszencia</td><td>At least 450 g/L, at only a few percent of alcohol</td></tr></table>
<p>Sugar alone is syrup. What makes these wines great is <b>acidity</b>: Riesling, Chenin Blanc, Furmint and Sémillon in a cool autumn keep enough acid to make 150 g/L taste fresh. When you assess a sweet wine, ask first whether the finish is clean or cloying.</p>
<h3>Why they cost so much</h3>
<p>Every method throws away water, and water is volume. Château d'Yquem averages about 9 hl/ha, often put as one glass of wine per vine, against 50 or more for a dry white. Pickers pass through a botrytized vineyard several times, berry by berry. Icewine growers leave fruit hanging into December for birds, rot and thaw to take. In poor years there is no wine at all.</p>
<h3>Telling the methods apart in the glass</h3>
<table class="t"><tr><th>Method</th><th>Signature</th></tr>
<tr><td>Noble rot</td><td>Honey, apricot, marmalade, saffron, ginger; a thick, glycerol-rich texture</td></tr>
<tr><td>Late harvest</td><td>Ripe and dried stone fruit, less honeyed, lighter</td></tr>
<tr><td>Icewine</td><td>Very pure fruit (peach, pineapple, lychee) with piercing acidity and no botrytis notes</td></tr>
<tr><td>Drying (passito)</td><td>Raisin, fig, dried apricot, caramel; often amber</td></tr>
<tr><td>Fortified during fermentation</td><td>Fresh grape sweetness with the warmth of spirit</td></tr></table>
<p>Freezing picked grapes in a freezer (<b>cryoextraction</b>) imitates icewine cheaply. Canada, Germany and Austria do not allow such wines to be called Icewine or Eiswein.</p>
<h3>The fortified map</h3>
<table class="t"><tr><th>Wine</th><th>Home</th><th>Fortified</th><th>Strength</th><th>Style</th></tr>
<tr><td>Port</td><td>Douro, Portugal</td><td>During fermentation</td><td>19 to 22%</td><td>Sweet; ruby (fruit) or tawny (wood-aged)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sherry</td><td>Jerez, Spain</td><td>After fermentation</td><td>15 to 22%</td><td>Dry by nature; sweetened styles exist</td></tr>
<tr><td>Madeira</td><td>Madeira, Portugal</td><td>During or after, by style</td><td>17 to 22%</td><td>Dry to sweet; heated and oxidized; very high acid</td></tr>
<tr><td>Marsala</td><td>Sicily</td><td>After</td><td>17 to 18% or more</td><td>Dry to sweet; oro, ambra, rubino</td></tr>
<tr><td>Vins Doux Naturels</td><td>Southern France</td><td>During</td><td>15 to 18%</td><td>Sweet Muscat or Grenache</td></tr>
<tr><td>Rutherglen Muscat</td><td>Victoria, Australia</td><td>During, very early</td><td>17 to 18%</td><td>Luscious, raisined, long wood age</td></tr></table>
<p>Madeira's four noble grapes run from dry to sweet: <b>Sercial, Verdelho, Boal, Malmsey</b>. Most everyday Madeira is Tinta Negra.</p>
<h3>Serving them</h3>
<ul><li><b>Pour:</b> 2 to 3 oz, in a small white-wine glass, not a thimble.</li>
<li><b>Temperature:</b> sweet whites and Fino well chilled; Tawny lightly chilled; Vintage Port at cool room temperature.</li>
<li><b>Once opened:</b> Fino and Manzanilla last days; Vintage Port a day or two; LBV and Tawny some weeks; Madeira months.</li>
<li><b>With food:</b> the wine must be sweeter than the dish, and salt is its best friend: blue cheese, foie gras, salted nuts.</li></ul>`,
floor:`<p>Fortified service: Port and Madeira are stored upright; vintage Port needs decanting. Fortified and dessert wines are a common CMS pairing and upselling topic: blue cheese and Sauternes or Port, chocolate and Banyuls or Maury.</p>`,
analyst:`<p>WSET Level 3 LO4 covers Port, Sherry, Muscat de Beaumes-de-Venise and Rutherglen Muscat. Madeira, Marsala and other VDNs are Diploma (D5) material. Fortified wines use their own alcohol scale on the SAT.</p>`,
terms:[["Süssreserve","Unfermented grape juice added for sweetness (Germany)."],["Passito","Wine from dried grapes (Italy)."],["Flor","Yeast film protecting Sherry from oxygen during biological aging."],["Estufagem","Heating Madeira in tanks."],["Canteiro","Aging Madeira casks in warm lofts."],["Vin Doux Naturel","French fortified wine with spirit added during fermentation."],["Cryoextraction","Freezing picked grapes artificially to concentrate the juice."],["Eszencia","The free-run syrup of Tokaji aszú berries: 450 g/L of sugar or more."],["Malmsey","The sweetest of Madeira's noble grapes (Malvasia)."],["Sercial","The driest of Madeira's noble grapes."]],
quiz:[
[1,"Port is fortified:","During fermentation, keeping natural sugar",["After fermentation, then sweetened","Before fermentation starts at all","At bottling, to stabilize the wine"],"Spirit kills the yeast, stopping fermentation."],
[1,"Sherry is fortified:","After fermentation is complete",["During fermentation, like Port","Before the grapes are pressed","Only when making sweet styles"],"Base wine is dry; sweet styles are blended or from PX."],
[2,"What happens if Sherry is fortified above about 17%?","Flor dies and the wine ages oxidatively",["Flor grows thicker and the wine pales","It can be sold only as Manzanilla","It must be sweetened with PX"],"That's the route to Oloroso."],
[1,"Icewine grapes must be:","Naturally frozen on the vine",["Frozen in a commercial freezer","Affected by noble rot","Dried on straw mats"],"Cryoextraction is not allowed for Icewine/Eiswein."],
[2,"Amarone is made from:","Dried grapes fermented to dryness",["Botrytized grapes picked late","Grapes frozen on the vine","Wine fortified during ferment"],"Recioto is the sweet sibling."],
[2,"Which wine is deliberately heated during aging?","Madeira",["Port","Sherry","Banyuls"],"Estufagem or canteiro."],
[3,"Rutherglen Muscat comes from:","Victoria, Australia",["Roussillon, France","Andalucía, Spain","Paarl, South Africa"],"Classification ladder: Rutherglen, Classic, Grand, Rare."],
[2,"What keeps a great sweet wine from tasting cloying?","High acidity",["High alcohol","New oak","Low temperature alone"],"Acid balances sugar: the reason Riesling, Chenin, Furmint and Sémillon dominate."],
[2,"Rutherglen Muscat is fortified:","Very early in fermentation",["After fermentation is complete","Only at bottling","Not at all"],"Almost all the grape sugar is kept, then the wine ages for years in old wood."],
[2,"Honey, saffron and marmalade with a thick texture point to:","Noble rot",["Icewine","Fortification","Carbonic maceration"],"Botrytis adds its own flavors; icewine shows pure fruit without them."],
[3,"Tokaji Aszú must contain at least:","120 g/L of sugar",["45 g/L of sugar","60 g/L of sugar","450 g/L of sugar"],"Eszencia starts at 450 g/L."],
[2,"A standard pour of dessert or fortified wine is about:","2 to 3 oz",["5 to 6 oz","1/2 oz","8 oz"],"Smaller than table wine, but in a real glass so the aromas can open."],
[2,"Freezing picked grapes artificially is called:","Cryoextraction",["Passerillage","Estufagem","Mutage"],"It imitates icewine but cannot use the name in Canada, Germany or Austria."]
]});

// ---------- F4 GRAPES ----------
L.push({id:"g1", unit:"F4", title:"The White Grapes", mins:18,
summary:"Structure, aromas and classic regions of the white varieties you must know cold.",
body:`
<p>For each grape, learn three things: its <b>structure</b> (acid, alcohol, body, phenolics), its <b>signature aromas</b>, and its <b>benchmark regions</b>. The Tasting Lab uses exactly these profiles.</p>
<table class="t"><tr><th>Grape</th><th>Structure</th><th>Markers</th><th>Benchmarks</th></tr>
<tr><td>Chardonnay</td><td>Chameleon: acid medium to high, body light to full</td><td>Cool: green apple, citrus, chalk. Warm: tropical, butter, vanilla (MLC, oak)</td><td>Chablis, Côte de Beaune, Mâconnais, Champagne, Sonoma, Napa, Margaret River</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sauvignon Blanc</td><td>High acid, light-medium body, usually no oak</td><td>Grass, gooseberry, grapefruit, flint (Loire); passion fruit, jalapeño (NZ). Pyrazines.</td><td>Sancerre, Pouilly-Fumé, Marlborough, Bordeaux (with Sémillon)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Riesling</td><td>Very high acid, any sweetness, often low alcohol</td><td>Lime, green apple, peach, white flowers, slate; petrol (TDN) with age</td><td>Mosel, Rheingau, Alsace, Wachau, Clare and Eden Valleys, Finger Lakes</td></tr>
<tr><td>Chenin Blanc</td><td>Very high acid, dry to sweet, sparkling too</td><td>Quince, baked apple, honey, wool/lanolin, chamomile</td><td>Vouvray, Savennières, Montlouis, Coteaux du Layon, Stellenbosch, Swartland</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sémillon</td><td>Low-medium acid, waxy, medium-full</td><td>Lemon, lanolin, beeswax; toast with age (Hunter Valley)</td><td>Sauternes, Graves/Pessac, Hunter Valley</td></tr>
<tr><td>Gewürztraminer</td><td>Low acid, high alcohol, oily, phenolic bitter finish</td><td>Lychee, rose, ginger, Turkish delight</td><td>Alsace, Alto Adige</td></tr>
<tr><td>Viognier</td><td>Low acid, high alcohol, full</td><td>Apricot, peach, honeysuckle</td><td>Condrieu, Château-Grillet; co-fermented in Côte-Rôtie</td></tr>
<tr><td>Pinot Grigio / Gris</td><td>Italy: light, neutral, crisp. Alsace: richer, off-dry</td><td>Pear, almond, lemon; smoky spice in Alsace</td><td>Friuli, Alto Adige, Alsace, Oregon</td></tr>
<tr><td>Albariño</td><td>High acid, light, no oak</td><td>Saline, peach, grapefruit, citrus blossom</td><td>Rías Baixas; as Alvarinho in Vinho Verde</td></tr>
<tr><td>Grüner Veltliner</td><td>Medium-high acid, light to full</td><td>White pepper, green bean, lentil, citrus</td><td>Wachau, Kamptal, Kremstal, Weinviertel</td></tr>
<tr><td>Torrontés</td><td>Medium acid, dry, phenolic bitterness</td><td>Very floral like Muscat (rose, orange blossom) but dry</td><td>Salta (Cafayate)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Muscat</td><td>Varies, often sweet or sparkling</td><td>Grapey, orange blossom, rose. The only grape that smells of grapes.</td><td>Asti, Beaumes-de-Venise, Rutherglen, Alsace</td></tr>
<tr><td>Melon (de Bourgogne)</td><td>High acid, light, neutral</td><td>Green apple, saline, bready from lees</td><td>Muscadet</td></tr>
<tr><td>Assyrtiko</td><td>Very high acid, can be full</td><td>Lemon, flint, saline</td><td>Santorini</td></tr>
<tr><td>Furmint</td><td>High acid</td><td>Quince, smoke, pear; apricot and marmalade in Aszú</td><td>Tokaj</td></tr></table>
<h3>Confusion pairs to drill</h3>
<ul><li><b>Vouvray vs German Riesling:</b> both high acid with RS. Chenin has honey, quince and lanolin, fuller body; Riesling is lime, slate, lighter, lower alcohol.</li>
<li><b>Chablis vs Sancerre:</b> both cool, high acid, mineral. Sancerre has green aromatics (grass, gooseberry); Chablis has green apple, lemon and chalk with no herbaceousness.</li>
<li><b>Gewürztraminer vs Torrontés:</b> both floral and bitter. Gewürz is lower acid, oilier, with lychee and rose; Torrontés is lighter, fresher, more citrus blossom.</li>
<li><b>California vs Côte d'Or Chardonnay:</b> riper tropical fruit, more alcohol and overt new oak in California; more acid, mineral and restraint in Burgundy.</li></ul>
<h3>Aromatic, semi-aromatic, neutral</h3>
<p>Sorting whites by how loudly the grape itself speaks is the fastest first step in a blind tasting.</p>
<table class="t"><tr><th>Family</th><th>Grapes</th><th>What it means</th></tr>
<tr><td>Aromatic</td><td>Muscat, Gewürztraminer, Torrontés, Riesling, Viognier, Sauvignon Blanc</td><td>The grape dominates. Usually no oak, no malolactic conversion.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Semi-aromatic</td><td>Chenin Blanc, Albariño, Grüner Veltliner, Pinot Gris, Sémillon</td><td>Clear character, open to some winemaking.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Neutral</td><td>Chardonnay, Melon, Pinot Blanc, Trebbiano, Garganega, Pinot Grigio</td><td>The winemaker dominates: oak, lees, malolactic conversion, bubbles.</td></tr></table>
<h3>The chemistry behind the smells</h3>
<table class="t"><tr><th>Compound</th><th>Smells of</th><th>Found in</th></tr>
<tr><td>Terpenes</td><td>Flowers, orange blossom, lychee, grapes</td><td>Muscat, Gewürztraminer, Torrontés, Riesling</td></tr>
<tr><td>Thiols</td><td>Grapefruit, passion fruit, boxwood</td><td>Sauvignon Blanc, above all Marlborough</td></tr>
<tr><td>Pyrazines</td><td>Cut grass, green pepper, asparagus</td><td>Sauvignon Blanc from cool sites</td></tr>
<tr><td>Rotundone</td><td>White pepper</td><td>Grüner Veltliner</td></tr>
<tr><td>TDN</td><td>Petrol, kerosene</td><td>Riesling with age or sun exposure</td></tr></table>
<h3>One grape, many places</h3>
<table class="t"><tr><th>Grape</th><th>From leanest to richest</th></tr>
<tr><td>Chardonnay</td><td>Chablis (steel, green apple, chalk), Côte de Beaune (hazelnut, restrained oak), Mâcon (riper, soft), Sonoma Coast and Margaret River (citrus and oak, bright acid), Napa (tropical, buttery, full)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Riesling</td><td>Mosel Kabinett (8%, sweet-tart, slate), Rheingau and Nahe dry Grosses Gewächs (12.5%, stony), Alsace (dry, fuller, 13%), Wachau Smaragd (ripe, powerful), Clare and Eden Valleys (bone dry, lime, early petrol)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sauvignon Blanc</td><td>Sancerre (restrained, flinty), Marlborough (pungent, passion fruit), Pessac-Léognan (oaked, blended with Sémillon), Napa Fumé Blanc (riper, sometimes oaked)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Chenin Blanc</td><td>Savennières (dry, austere), Vouvray (dry to sweet, sparkling), Coteaux du Layon (botrytized), Swartland (old vines, dry, textured)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Pinot Gris</td><td>Veneto Pinot Grigio (light, neutral), Alto Adige and Friuli (more texture), Oregon (pear, medium body), Alsace (rich, smoky, often off-dry)</td></tr></table>
<h3>Which whites age</h3>
<p>Acid, sugar and concentration preserve white wine. Riesling, Chenin Blanc, Hunter Sémillon, Furmint and top Chardonnay can improve for decades, turning toward honey, toast, nuts and petrol. Most Sauvignon Blanc, Pinot Grigio, Albariño, Torrontés and Muscat are at their best within two or three years. Exceptions prove skill: aged Pessac-Léognan, and Sancerre from the best growers.</p>
<h3>More pairs to drill</h3>
<ul><li><b>Albariño vs Grüner Veltliner vs Pinot Grigio:</b> all light and crisp. Albariño is peachy and salty; Grüner has white pepper and a lentil-like savor; Pinot Grigio is the most neutral, with a faint almond bitterness.</li>
<li><b>Viognier vs Gewürztraminer:</b> both low in acid and oily. Viognier is apricot and honeysuckle; Gewürztraminer is lychee, rose and ginger, usually with a touch of sugar.</li>
<li><b>Dry Riesling, three ways:</b> Australia is lime cordial and bone dry; Alsace is broader and riper; Austria adds stone fruit and spice with more alcohol.</li>
<li><b>Savennières vs white Burgundy:</b> similar weight. Chenin shows quince, wax and bruised apple with sharper acid; Chardonnay shows lemon, hazelnut and oak.</li></ul>`,
floor:`<p>These are the white grapes on the CMS Americas Certified examinable list: Albariño, Chardonnay (Chablis, Côte de Beaune, Mâconnais, California), Chenin Blanc (Vouvray), Gewurztraminer (Alsace), Pinot Grigio (Friuli/Alto Adige), Riesling (Mosel/Rheingau Kabinett or Spätlese), Sauvignon Blanc (Sancerre/Pouilly-Fumé, Marlborough), Torrontés (Salta/Mendoza).</p>`,
analyst:`<p>WSET wants the "why": Riesling's acid survives because it ripens slowly in cool sites; Viognier is low acid because it needs warmth to develop aroma and loses acid as it does. Always link structure to cause.</p>`,
terms:[["TDN","Compound giving petrol aroma in aged Riesling."],["Pyrazines","Green bell pepper and grassy compounds (Sauvignon Blanc, Cabernets)."],["Alvarinho","Portuguese name for Albariño."],["Terpenes","Floral aroma compounds of Muscat, Gewürztraminer and Riesling."],["Thiols","Sulfur-based aromas of grapefruit and passion fruit, typical of Sauvignon Blanc."],["Rotundone","The pepper compound in Grüner Veltliner and Syrah."],["Aromatic variety","A grape whose own aroma dominates the wine, such as Muscat."]],
quiz:[
[1,"Lychee and rose with low acid and an oily texture suggest:","Gewürztraminer",["Riesling","Albariño","Sauvignon Blanc"],"Classic Alsace Gewurztraminer markers."],
[1,"Petrol aroma in an aged white points to:","Riesling",["Chardonnay","Viognier","Pinot Grigio"],"TDN develops with age and sun exposure."],
[2,"Quince, honey and lanolin with high acid and slight sweetness suggests:","Vouvray (Chenin Blanc)",["Mosel Kabinett (Riesling)","Sancerre (Sauvignon Blanc)","Chablis (Chardonnay)"],"The CMS-recommended confusion pair is Vouvray vs German Riesling."],
[1,"White pepper and green bean are signatures of:","Grüner Veltliner",["Chenin Blanc","Muscat","Sémillon"],"Austria's flagship white."],
[2,"Which grape is a dry, floral Argentine white with a bitter finish?","Torrontés",["Gewürztraminer","Viognier","Muscat"],"Highest-quality examples from Cafayate, Salta."],
[1,"Viognier's home appellation in the Northern Rhône is:","Condrieu",["Hermitage","Cornas","Sancerre"],"Château-Grillet is a tiny monopole enclave within it."],
[2,"Unaged Hunter Valley Sémillon is typically:","Low alcohol, high acid and lemony",["Full, oaked and buttery","Sweet, golden and botrytized","Aromatic, oily and low in acid"],"Picked early at 10-11.5% alcohol and never oaked; toast and honey appear only with years in bottle."],
[3,"Assyrtiko is native to:","Santorini",["Crete","Cyprus","Sicily"],"Ungrafted, basket-trained (kouloura) vines in volcanic soils."],
[2,"Which grape is 'neutral', so that winemaking sets its style?","Chardonnay",["Gewürztraminer","Muscat","Sauvignon Blanc"],"Neutral grapes take on oak, lees and malolactic character."],
[2,"Lychee and orange-blossom aromas in Gewürztraminer and Muscat come from:","Terpenes",["Thiols","Pyrazines","Rotundone"],"Thiols give grapefruit and passion fruit; pyrazines give green notes; rotundone gives pepper."],
[2,"Which white grape's wines usually should be drunk within two or three years?","Pinot Grigio",["Riesling","Chenin Blanc","Furmint"],"Acid, sugar and concentration preserve whites; light neutral wines fade."],
[2,"Which Riesling is typically bone dry with lime and early petrol?","Clare Valley",["Mosel Kabinett","Alsace Vendange Tardive","Rheingau Auslese"],"Clare and Eden Valley Rieslings are dry, lime-scented and high in acid."],
[3,"Apricot and honeysuckle with low acid and no lychee point to:","Viognier",["Gewürztraminer","Torrontés","Muscat"],"Gewürztraminer shows lychee, rose and ginger."],
[2,"The white-pepper note in Grüner Veltliner comes from:","Rotundone",["Pyrazines","Diacetyl","Thiols"],"The same compound gives black pepper in Syrah."]
]});

L.push({id:"g2", unit:"F4", title:"The Red Grapes", mins:20,
summary:"Structure, markers and benchmarks for the red varieties on every exam.",
body:`
<table class="t"><tr><th>Grape</th><th>Structure</th><th>Markers</th><th>Benchmarks</th></tr>
<tr><td>Cabernet Sauvignon</td><td>Deep, high tannin, medium+ acid, full</td><td>Cassis, blackberry, cedar, graphite, mint, green pepper (cool)</td><td>Médoc, Graves, Napa, Coonawarra, Margaret River, Maipo, Bolgheri</td></tr>
<tr><td>Merlot</td><td>Medium-high tannin, softer, medium acid</td><td>Plum, black cherry, cocoa, fruitcake</td><td>Pomerol, Saint-Émilion, Napa, Washington</td></tr>
<tr><td>Cabernet Franc</td><td>Medium body, medium+ acid, medium tannin</td><td>Raspberry, red currant, green pepper, violet, graphite</td><td>Chinon, Bourgueil, Saumur-Champigny, Saint-Émilion (Cheval Blanc)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Pinot Noir</td><td>Pale, high acid, low-medium tannin</td><td>Red cherry, raspberry, forest floor, mushroom, rose</td><td>Côte d'Or, Champagne, Willamette, Sonoma Coast, Central Otago, Martinborough</td></tr>
<tr><td>Syrah / Shiraz</td><td>Deep, medium+ tannin, medium acid</td><td>France: black pepper, olive, smoked meat, violet. Australia: jammy black fruit, eucalyptus, chocolate</td><td>Côte-Rôtie, Hermitage, Cornas, Saint-Joseph, Barossa, McLaren Vale</td></tr>
<tr><td>Grenache / Garnacha</td><td>Pale-medium, high alcohol, low acid, soft</td><td>Kirsch, strawberry, garrigue, white pepper</td><td>Châteauneuf-du-Pape, Gigondas, Priorat, Barossa</td></tr>
<tr><td>Mourvèdre / Monastrell</td><td>Deep, high tannin</td><td>Blackberry, game, meat, leather</td><td>Bandol, Jumilla, GSM blends</td></tr>
<tr><td>Malbec</td><td>Opaque purple, medium+ tannin</td><td>Blueberry, plum, violet; purple rim</td><td>Mendoza (Luján de Cuyo, Uco Valley), Cahors</td></tr>
<tr><td>Carmenère</td><td>Medium+ body</td><td>Black fruit, green pepper, paprika</td><td>Chile</td></tr>
<tr><td>Tempranillo</td><td>Medium acid and tannin</td><td>Red cherry, leather, tobacco; dill and coconut from American oak</td><td>Rioja, Ribera del Duero, Toro; Tinta Roriz in Douro</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sangiovese</td><td>High acid, medium+ tannin, medium-pale</td><td>Sour cherry, tomato leaf, dried herbs, tea, earth</td><td>Chianti Classico, Brunello di Montalcino, Vino Nobile</td></tr>
<tr><td>Nebbiolo</td><td>Pale garnet, high acid, high tannin</td><td>Tar and roses, cherry, violet, licorice, dried herbs</td><td>Barolo, Barbaresco, Roero, Valtellina, Gattinara</td></tr>
<tr><td>Barbera</td><td>Deep, high acid, low tannin</td><td>Sour cherry, plum</td><td>Asti, Alba</td></tr>
<tr><td>Gamay</td><td>Pale-medium, low tannin, high acid</td><td>Banana, bubblegum, red berries (carbonic)</td><td>Beaujolais and its crus</td></tr>
<tr><td>Zinfandel / Primitivo</td><td>High alcohol, medium+ body</td><td>Jammy bramble, raisin, black pepper</td><td>Sonoma (Dry Creek), Lodi, Paso Robles; Puglia</td></tr>
<tr><td>Pinotage</td><td>Medium+ tannin</td><td>Black fruit, smoke, banana or acetone notes</td><td>Stellenbosch</td></tr></table>
<h3>Confusion pairs to drill</h3>
<ul><li><b>Pinot Noir vs Nebbiolo:</b> both pale. Nebbiolo's tannin and acid are far higher; tar and dried roses versus fresh red cherry and forest floor.</li>
<li><b>Sangiovese vs Nebbiolo:</b> Sangiovese is darker, less tannic, with tomato leaf and sour cherry.</li>
<li><b>Sangiovese vs Rioja:</b> Rioja shows dill/coconut American oak, leather, lower acid.</li>
<li><b>Grenache vs Rioja:</b> Grenache is higher alcohol, lighter color, kirsch and garrigue; Rioja shows oak and leather.</li>
<li><b>Shiraz vs Malbec vs Zinfandel:</b> Shiraz has eucalyptus and chocolate, Malbec violet and a purple rim, Zinfandel jammy bramble and high alcohol, sometimes a hint of raisin.</li>
<li><b>Burgundy vs US Pinot:</b> earth, acid and restraint versus riper dark cherry, cola and more alcohol.</li></ul>
<h3>Skin thickness explains color and tannin</h3>
<table class="t"><tr><th>Skins</th><th>Grapes</th><th>In the glass</th></tr>
<tr><td>Thin</td><td>Pinot Noir, Gamay, Grenache, Nebbiolo, Sangiovese</td><td>Pale to medium color. (Nebbiolo is the exception that is pale but very tannic.)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Medium</td><td>Merlot, Tempranillo, Cabernet Franc, Zinfandel, Barbera</td><td>Medium to deep ruby</td></tr>
<tr><td>Thick</td><td>Cabernet Sauvignon, Syrah, Malbec, Mourvèdre, Tannat, Petite Sirah</td><td>Deep to opaque, with more tannin</td></tr></table>
<p>If you can read text through the glass, cross the thick-skinned grapes off your list before you smell the wine.</p>
<h3>Marker compounds</h3>
<table class="t"><tr><th>Marker</th><th>Smells of</th><th>Points to</th></tr>
<tr><td>Pyrazines</td><td>Green pepper, leaf, tomato stalk</td><td>The Cabernet family: Cabernet Sauvignon, Cabernet Franc, Carmenère, cool-climate Merlot</td></tr>
<tr><td>Rotundone</td><td>Black pepper</td><td>Syrah from cool sites</td></tr>
<tr><td>Eucalyptol</td><td>Mint, eucalyptus</td><td>Australia, also Napa and Chile, from nearby trees</td></tr>
<tr><td>Carbonic esters</td><td>Banana, bubblegum, kirsch</td><td>Beaujolais</td></tr>
<tr><td>American oak lactones</td><td>Coconut, dill</td><td>Rioja, some Zinfandel and Barossa Shiraz</td></tr></table>
<h3>Who does what in a blend</h3>
<table class="t"><tr><th>Blend</th><th>Roles</th></tr>
<tr><td>Bordeaux</td><td>Cabernet Sauvignon: structure, cassis, aging. Merlot: flesh and softness. Cabernet Franc: perfume. Petit Verdot: color and spice.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Southern Rhône (GSM)</td><td>Grenache: fruit, alcohol, body. Syrah: color, pepper, structure. Mourvèdre: tannin, game, aging.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Rioja</td><td>Tempranillo: backbone. Garnacha: body. Graciano: acid and aroma. Mazuelo: tannin.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Chianti Classico</td><td>Sangiovese (at least 80%) with Canaiolo, Colorino or international grapes.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Valpolicella</td><td>Corvina and Corvinone: cherry and structure. Rondinella: color and reliability.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Port</td><td>Touriga Nacional: perfume and tannin. Touriga Franca: finesse. Tinta Roriz: body.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Champagne</td><td>Pinot Noir: body. Meunier: fruit. Chardonnay: acidity and finesse.</td></tr></table>
<h3>One grape, many places</h3>
<table class="t"><tr><th>Grape</th><th>From leanest to richest</th></tr>
<tr><td>Pinot Noir</td><td>Germany and Alsace (pale, light), Burgundy (earth, sour cherry), Oregon (cranberry, cherry, earth), Central Otago (dark cherry, thyme), Sonoma and Santa Barbara (ripe, cola, more alcohol)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Syrah</td><td>Northern Rhône (pepper, olive, 12.5 to 13.5%), Washington and Hawke's Bay (between), Barossa (jammy, chocolate, 14.5% and up)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Cabernet Sauvignon</td><td>Médoc (graphite, cedar, firm), Coonawarra (cassis, mint), Maipo (cassis, eucalyptus, earth), Napa (ripe black fruit, plush tannin, new oak)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Malbec</td><td>Cahors (dark, firm, earthy), Mendoza (plush, violet, sweet fruit)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Tempranillo</td><td>Rioja (red fruit, American oak, medium body), Ribera del Duero (darker, more tannin, French oak), Toro (the biggest and ripest)</td></tr></table>
<h3>How reds change with age</h3>
<p>Color moves from purple to ruby to garnet to tawny, and the rim pales first. Tannin softens and falls out as sediment. Fresh fruit turns to dried fruit, then to savory notes:</p>
<ul><li><b>Cabernet Sauvignon:</b> cedar, cigar box, tobacco, pencil shavings.</li>
<li><b>Pinot Noir:</b> mushroom, forest floor, game.</li>
<li><b>Nebbiolo:</b> tar, leather, truffle, dried rose.</li>
<li><b>Syrah:</b> leather, smoked meat, olive.</li>
<li><b>Sangiovese:</b> dried cherry, leather, balsamic.</li>
<li><b>Tempranillo:</b> leather, tobacco, dried fig.</li></ul>
<h3>More pairs to drill</h3>
<ul><li><b>Cabernet Sauvignon vs Merlot vs Cabernet Franc:</b> Cabernet Sauvignon is the darkest and firmest, with cassis. Merlot is rounder, with plum and softer tannin. Cabernet Franc is paler and lighter, with raspberry, violet and pencil lead.</li>
<li><b>Malbec vs Syrah:</b> both dark. Malbec is plush and sweet-fruited with violet; Syrah is more savory, with pepper, olive or smoked meat.</li>
<li><b>Ribera del Duero vs Bordeaux:</b> both dark, structured and oaked. Ribera has riper fruit and softer acid; Bordeaux shows graphite and a green edge.</li></ul>`,
floor:`<p>CMS Americas Certified red list: Cabernet Franc (Chinon/Bourgueil), Cabernet Sauvignon (Haut-Médoc/Graves; Napa/Sonoma/Paso), Gamay (Beaujolais), Grenache (Châteauneuf/Gigondas), Malbec (Mendoza), Merlot (Pomerol/St-Émilion; Napa/Sonoma), Nebbiolo (Barolo/Barbaresco), Pinot Noir (Côte d'Or; Carneros/Sonoma/Central Coast; Willamette), Sangiovese (Brunello/Chianti Classico), Syrah/Shiraz (Northern Rhône; South Australia/WA/Victoria), Tempranillo (Rioja Reserva/Gran Reserva), Zinfandel (Napa/Sonoma).</p>`,
analyst:`<p>WSET short answers love red grape "why" questions: why Nebbiolo is pale yet tannic (thin skins low in stable anthocyanins, but rich in tannin), why Pinot Noir struggles in hot climates (thin skins, early ripening, loses aroma and acid).</p>`,
terms:[["GSM","Grenache, Syrah, Mourvèdre blend."],["Tinta Roriz","Portuguese name for Tempranillo."],["Primitivo","Italian name for Zinfandel (both are Croatian Tribidrag)."],["Eucalyptol","Compound from nearby eucalyptus trees that gives a minty note."],["Petit Verdot","Late-ripening Bordeaux grape adding color and spice to blends."],["Tertiary aromas","Aromas of age: leather, tobacco, mushroom, dried fruit."],["Corvinone","Partner of Corvina in Valpolicella and Amarone."]],
quiz:[
[1,"Pale garnet color with high tannin, high acid, tar and roses suggests:","Nebbiolo",["Pinot Noir","Grenache","Gamay"],"The classic Barolo profile."],
[1,"Black pepper, olive and smoked meat suggest:","Northern Rhône Syrah",["Australian Shiraz","Malbec","Merlot"],"Rotundone gives black pepper."],
[2,"Dill and coconut with leather in a medium-bodied red point to:","Rioja Reserva (Tempranillo)",["Chianti Classico (Sangiovese)","Barolo (Nebbiolo)","Pomerol (Merlot)"],"American oak aging is traditional in Rioja."],
[1,"Banana and bubblegum in a light red point to:","Gamay from Beaujolais",["Pinot Noir from Burgundy","Nebbiolo from Piedmont","Barbera from Asti"],"Carbonic maceration esters."],
[2,"Which red is known for high acid but low tannin?","Barbera",["Nebbiolo","Cabernet Sauvignon","Mourvèdre"],"Barbera's low tannin is why it pairs widely."],
[2,"Zinfandel is genetically identical to:","Primitivo",["Petite Sirah","Grenache","Carignan"],"Both are Croatian Crljenak Kaštelanski / Tribidrag."],
[2,"Which grape gives a purple rim, blueberry and violet?","Malbec",["Nebbiolo","Grenache","Sangiovese"],"Mendoza Malbec's color is a giveaway."],
[3,"Carmenère was long mistaken in Chile for:","Merlot",["Cabernet Franc","Malbec","Syrah"],"Identified by ampelographer Jean-Michel Boursiquot in 1994."],
[2,"Which grape has thin skins and usually makes pale wine?","Grenache",["Malbec","Tannat","Petite Sirah"],"Pinot Noir, Gamay, Grenache, Nebbiolo and Sangiovese are thin-skinned."],
[2,"In a Bordeaux blend, Merlot mainly adds:","Flesh and softness",["Acidity and perfume","Color and spice","Tannin and cassis"],"Cabernet Sauvignon gives structure; Cabernet Franc perfume; Petit Verdot color and spice."],
[2,"In a GSM blend, which grape supplies most tannin and gamey depth?","Mourvèdre",["Grenache","Cinsault","Syrah"],"Grenache gives fruit and alcohol; Syrah color and pepper."],
[2,"Mint or eucalyptus in a Cabernet Sauvignon most suggests:","Coonawarra",["Pauillac","Bolgheri","Pomerol"],"Eucalyptol comes from nearby trees: Australia, sometimes Napa and Chile."],
[2,"With age, Cabernet Sauvignon develops:","Cedar and cigar box",["Banana and bubblegum","Lychee and rose","Mushroom and game only"],"Mushroom and forest floor are the classic notes of aged Pinot Noir."],
[3,"Compared with Rioja, Ribera del Duero is usually:","Darker, firmer, with French oak",["Paler and lighter in body","Lower in alcohol and tannin","Made mainly from Garnacha"],"Both are Tempranillo; Ribera's altitude and extremes give thicker skins."],
[2,"As a red wine ages, its color moves toward:","Garnet, then tawny",["Purple, then ruby","Deeper ruby, then purple","Ruby, then blue-black"],"Whites deepen with age; reds lose color and turn brick at the rim."]
]});

L.push({id:"g3", unit:"F4", title:"Important Regional Grapes", mins:12,
summary:"The supporting cast that shows up on Certified and Advanced theory papers.",
body:`
<p>These grapes matter less for blind tasting at Certified but appear constantly in theory questions, especially matching exercises (grape to appellation).</p>
<h3>Whites</h3>
<ul><li><b>Garganega:</b> Soave. Almond, lemon, pear.</li><li><b>Verdicchio:</b> Castelli di Jesi and Matelica (Marche). Bitter almond, citrus, high acid.</li><li><b>Fiano:</b> Fiano di Avellino DOCG. Waxy, hazelnut, honey.</li><li><b>Greco:</b> Greco di Tufo DOCG. Peach, almond.</li><li><b>Cortese:</b> Gavi. Lean, citrus.</li><li><b>Arneis:</b> Roero. Pear, almond.</li><li><b>Vermentino:</b> Sardinia (Vermentino di Gallura DOCG), Liguria; Rolle in Provence.</li><li><b>Glera:</b> Prosecco.</li><li><b>Trebbiano / Ugni Blanc:</b> neutral, high-yield; the base for Cognac and Armagnac.</li><li><b>Marsanne and Roussanne:</b> white Hermitage, Saint-Joseph, Saint-Péray.</li><li><b>Palomino:</b> Sherry. <b>Pedro Ximénez:</b> sweet Sherry, Montilla-Moriles.</li><li><b>Verdejo:</b> Rueda. <b>Macabeo (Viura), Xarel·lo, Parellada:</b> Cava.</li><li><b>Godello:</b> Valdeorras. <b>Loureiro:</b> Vinho Verde.</li><li><b>Pinot Blanc, Sylvaner, Muscat:</b> Alsace.</li><li><b>Silvaner:</b> Franken (in the Bocksbeutel flask).</li><li><b>Savagnin:</b> Jura, Vin Jaune.</li></ul>
<h3>Reds</h3>
<ul><li><b>Corvina, Rondinella, Molinara:</b> Valpolicella, Amarone, Bardolino.</li><li><b>Dolcetto:</b> Dogliani, Alba. Low acid, grippy, bitter almond.</li><li><b>Aglianico:</b> Taurasi (Campania), Aglianico del Vulture (Basilicata). High acid and tannin.</li><li><b>Nero d'Avola:</b> Sicily. <b>Nerello Mascalese:</b> Etna. <b>Frappato:</b> Cerasuolo di Vittoria.</li><li><b>Montepulciano:</b> Montepulciano d'Abruzzo (not to be confused with Vino Nobile di Montepulciano, which is Sangiovese).</li><li><b>Lagrein, Schiava:</b> Alto Adige. <b>Teroldego:</b> Trentino.</li><li><b>Mencía:</b> Bierzo, Ribeira Sacra. <b>Graciano, Mazuelo (Carignan):</b> Rioja blending.</li><li><b>Touriga Nacional, Touriga Franca, Tinta Roriz, Tinta Barroca, Tinto Cão:</b> Port and Douro.</li><li><b>Baga:</b> Bairrada.</li><li><b>Xinomavro:</b> Naoussa ("acid-black", Nebbiolo-like). <b>Agiorgitiko:</b> Nemea.</li><li><b>Blaufränkisch, Zweigelt, St. Laurent:</b> Austria. Blaufränkisch is Kékfrankos in Hungary and Lemberger in Washington.</li><li><b>Tannat:</b> Madiran, Uruguay. <b>Petit Verdot:</b> Bordeaux blending.</li><li><b>Cinsault, Carignan:</b> Languedoc, Southern Rhône. <b>Pinot Meunier:</b> Champagne.</li><li><b>Trousseau, Poulsard:</b> Jura.</li></ul>
<h3>The regional grapes examiners pour blind</h3>
<p>From the Advanced exam upward, flights reach beyond the classics. These are the regional grapes most likely to appear, with the clue that separates each from its neighbor.</p>
<table class="t"><tr><th>Grape</th><th>Structure</th><th>Markers</th><th>Don't confuse with</th></tr>
<tr><td>Aglianico</td><td>Deep, very high tannin and acid</td><td>Black cherry, smoke, leather, ash</td><td>Nebbiolo (paler, more floral)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Nerello Mascalese</td><td>Pale, high acid, firm tannin</td><td>Sour cherry, herbs, volcanic smoke</td><td>Pinot Noir (softer), Nebbiolo (more tannic)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Xinomavro</td><td>Pale, high acid, high tannin</td><td>Tomato, olive, dried rose</td><td>Nebbiolo (tar, less tomato)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Mencía</td><td>Medium body, fresh acid, moderate tannin</td><td>Red fruit, violet, crushed stone, a herbal edge</td><td>Cabernet Franc, cru Beaujolais</td></tr>
<tr><td>Blaufränkisch</td><td>Deep color, high acid, firm tannin</td><td>Blackberry, sour cherry, black pepper</td><td>Syrah (lower acid)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Zweigelt</td><td>Medium body, soft tannin</td><td>Bright cherry, cinnamon</td><td>Gamay</td></tr>
<tr><td>Touriga Nacional</td><td>Deep, high tannin</td><td>Violet, bergamot, black fruit</td><td>Syrah, Malbec</td></tr>
<tr><td>Tannat</td><td>Opaque, massive tannin, high acid</td><td>Blackberry, licorice</td><td>Cabernet Sauvignon (less brutal)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Corvina</td><td>Light to medium, high acid, low tannin</td><td>Sour cherry, almond; dried fruit in Amarone</td><td>Sangiovese (more tannin)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Garganega</td><td>Medium body, medium acid</td><td>Almond, lemon, chamomile</td><td>Pinot Grigio (simpler)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Verdejo</td><td>Medium body, bitter finish</td><td>Fennel, bay leaf, grapefruit</td><td>Sauvignon Blanc (sharper, greener)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Godello</td><td>Medium to full, fresh acid</td><td>Apple, peach, wet stone; often lees or oak</td><td>Chardonnay</td></tr>
<tr><td>Marsanne and Roussanne</td><td>Full, low acid, oily</td><td>Pear, almond paste, honey, herbal tea</td><td>Viognier (more perfumed)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Fiano</td><td>Medium to full, waxy</td><td>Hazelnut, honey, smoke</td><td>Chenin Blanc (higher acid)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Savagnin</td><td>High acid; nutty under flor</td><td>Walnut, curry spice, green apple</td><td>Fino Sherry (lighter, lower acid)</td></tr></table>
<h3>Appellation matching: France</h3>
<p><b>Aligoté:</b> Bouzeron. <b>Jacquère</b> and <b>Altesse</b> (Roussette): Savoie whites; <b>Mondeuse:</b> Savoie red. <b>Petit Manseng</b> and <b>Gros Manseng:</b> Jurançon. <b>Mauzac:</b> Limoux and Gaillac. <b>Picpoul:</b> Picpoul de Pinet. <b>Clairette:</b> Clairette du Languedoc and Southern Rhône whites (the trap: Clairette de Die Tradition is mostly Muscat). <b>Négrette:</b> Fronton. <b>Fer Servadou:</b> Marcillac. <b>Romorantin:</b> Cour-Cheverny. <b>Grolleau:</b> Rosé d'Anjou. <b>Pineau d'Aunis:</b> Coteaux du Loir. <b>Sciaccarello</b> and <b>Nielluccio</b> (Sangiovese): Corsica. <b>Folle Blanche</b> and <b>Colombard:</b> Armagnac. <b>Chasselas:</b> Pouilly-sur-Loire, and Switzerland, where the Valais calls it Fendant.</p>
<h3>Appellation matching: Italy</h3>
<p><b>Sagrantino:</b> Montefalco. <b>Cesanese:</b> del Piglio. <b>Negroamaro:</b> Salice Salentino. <b>Gaglioppo:</b> Cirò. <b>Cannonau</b> (Grenache) and <b>Carignano del Sulcis:</b> Sardinia. <b>Refosco</b>, <b>Schioppettino</b>, <b>Friulano</b> and <b>Ribolla Gialla:</b> Friuli. <b>Ruchè</b>, <b>Grignolino</b>, <b>Freisa</b>, <b>Pelaverga:</b> Piedmont's other reds. <b>Brachetto:</b> Brachetto d'Acqui, sweet red and sparkling. <b>Lambrusco</b> (Sorbara, Grasparossa, Salamino): Emilia. <b>Vernaccia:</b> San Gimignano. <b>Pecorino</b> and <b>Passerina:</b> Marche and Abruzzo. <b>Falanghina:</b> Campania. <b>Carricante:</b> Etna Bianco. <b>Grillo</b> and <b>Catarratto:</b> western Sicily and Marsala. <b>Nosiola:</b> Trentino.</p>
<h3>Appellation matching: Iberia</h3>
<p><b>Bobal:</b> Utiel-Requena. <b>Monastrell:</b> Jumilla, Yecla. <b>Hondarrabi Zuri:</b> Txakoli. <b>Treixadura:</b> Ribeiro. <b>Listán Negro</b> and <b>Listán Blanco</b> (Palomino): Canary Islands. <b>Trepat:</b> Conca de Barberà rosé and Cava. <b>Encruzado:</b> Dão white. <b>Arinto:</b> Bucelas, and acidity for blends everywhere. <b>Alvarinho</b> and <b>Loureiro:</b> Vinho Verde. <b>Castelão</b> and <b>Trincadeira:</b> southern Portugal. <b>Alicante Bouschet:</b> Alentejo. <b>Tinta Negra:</b> Madeira.</p>
<h3>Appellation matching: Central and Eastern Europe</h3>
<p><b>Saperavi</b> and <b>Rkatsiteli:</b> Georgia. <b>Plavac Mali:</b> Dalmatia (Dingač, Postup). <b>Graševina</b> (Welschriesling): Slavonia. <b>Kadarka</b> and <b>Kékfrankos:</b> Hungary (Szekszárd, Eger). <b>Hárslevelű:</b> Tokaj, with Furmint. <b>Moschofilero:</b> Mantinia. <b>Malagousia</b> and <b>Robola:</b> Greek whites. <b>Trollinger</b> (Schiava) and <b>Lemberger:</b> Württemberg. <b>Dornfelder:</b> Pfalz and Rheinhessen. <b>Müller-Thurgau:</b> everyday Germany. <b>Chasselas</b> and <b>Petite Arvine:</b> Switzerland.</p>`,
floor:`<p>CMS Certified matching item example: pair Valpolicella, Bandol, Tokaj and Gigondas with Corvina, Mourvèdre, Furmint and Grenache. Practice these as fast lookups.</p>`,
analyst:`<p>Many of these (Garganega, Fiano, Aglianico, Xinomavro, Agiorgitiko, Assyrtiko) are named in the WSET Level 3 specification. Xinomavro (Naoussa), Agiorgitiko (Nemea) and Assyrtiko (Santorini) are the three Greek wines on the Level 3 list.</p>`,
terms:[["Garganega","White grape of Soave."],["Aglianico","Tannic southern Italian red; Taurasi, Vulture."],["Mencía","Red of Bierzo and Ribeira Sacra."],["Xinomavro","Tannic, high-acid red of Naoussa, Greece."],["Blaufränkisch","Austrian red; Kékfrankos in Hungary, Lemberger in the US."],["Sagrantino","Very tannic red grape of Montefalco in Umbria."],["Petit Manseng","Thick-skinned grape of Jurançon, used for sweet wines."],["Plavac Mali","Dalmatia's main red grape, an offspring of Zinfandel."],["Saperavi","Georgia's red-fleshed, deeply colored red grape."],["Carricante","White grape of Etna Bianco."],["Encruzado","The quality white grape of the Dão."]],
quiz:[
[1,"Match Valpolicella with its main grape:","Corvina",["Mourvèdre","Furmint","Grenache"],"From the official CMS matching example."],
[1,"Match Bandol with its main grape:","Mourvèdre",["Corvina","Furmint","Grenache"],"Bandol reds require at least 50% Mourvèdre."],
[2,"Montepulciano d'Abruzzo is made from:","The Montepulciano grape",["The Sangiovese grape","The Nebbiolo grape","The Aglianico grape"],"Vino Nobile di Montepulciano (Tuscany) is Sangiovese, named for the town."],
[2,"Which white grape is the base of Soave?","Garganega",["Cortese","Arneis","Verdicchio"],"Gavi is Cortese; Roero Arneis is Arneis."],
[2,"Cava's three traditional grapes are:","Macabeo, Xarel·lo, Parellada",["Palomino, PX, Moscatel","Verdejo, Viura, Godello","Albariño, Loureiro, Treixadura"],"Chardonnay and others are also permitted now."],
[2,"Xinomavro is the grape of:","Naoussa",["Nemea","Santorini","Tokaj"],"Agiorgitiko is Nemea; Assyrtiko is Santorini."],
[3,"Taurasi DOCG is made from:","Aglianico",["Nero d'Avola","Primitivo","Negroamaro"],"Sometimes called the Barolo of the South."],
[3,"Lemberger in Washington State is the same grape as:","Blaufränkisch",["Zweigelt","St. Laurent","Dornfelder"],"Kékfrankos in Hungary."],
[2,"Sagrantino is the grape of:","Montefalco",["Montalcino","Montepulciano","Morellino di Scansano"],"An Umbrian red with some of Italy's highest tannin. The other three are Sangiovese."],
[2,"Picpoul de Pinet comes from:","The Languedoc",["The Loire","Savoie","Alsace"],"A crisp, saline white from near the Étang de Thau."],
[2,"Jurançon is made mainly from:","Petit Manseng and Gros Manseng",["Mauzac and Chenin","Jacquère and Altesse","Sémillon and Muscadelle"],"Petit Manseng's thick skins let it shrivel on the vine for sweet wines."],
[3,"Pale color, high acid, firm tannin and a smoky, sour-cherry nose from a volcano suggest:","Nerello Mascalese",["Aglianico","Nero d'Avola","Primitivo"],"Etna Rosso. Aglianico is deep-colored."],
[2,"Hondarrabi Zuri is the grape of:","Txakoli",["Rías Baixas","Rueda","Ribeiro"],"The sharp, lightly sparkling white of the Basque coast."],
[2,"Plavac Mali is grown in:","Dalmatia, Croatia",["Georgia","Northern Greece","Slovenia"],"Dingač and Postup on the Pelješac peninsula. It is an offspring of Zinfandel."],
[2,"Bobal is the main grape of:","Utiel-Requena",["Bierzo","Priorat","Toro"],"A dark, fresh red from inland Valencia."],
[3,"Fennel and bay leaf with a bitter finish in a Spanish white suggest:","Verdejo",["Albariño","Godello","Viura"],"Rueda. Albariño is peachy and saline."]
]});
